Subject: [PATCH] qjs: a 256 KiB JS stack limit and 512 KiB of C stack on WASI

QuickJS checks its stack against a limit in linear memory (1 MiB by
default, inside upstream's 2 MiB WASI stack), but every C frame also takes
a frame on the wasm engine's own stack, which is much smaller in a
browser's process worker and on the stack a suspendable (JSPI) call runs
on in slicc-kernel's async process host. That stack ran out first: deep
recursion trapped (RangeError in wasm, the process ended with status 70)
instead of throwing a catchable RangeError. 512 KiB was still too much
in Chromium; with a 256 KiB default limit it throws in the browser, on
Node and in the async host. --stack-size still sets any other. The C
stack keeps upstream's 2:1 ratio to the limit at 512 KiB, which makes
the initial memory 1.5 MiB smaller.

--- a/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -173,7 +173,7 @@
         _WASI_EMULATED_SIGNAL
     )
     add_link_options(
-        -Wl,-z,stack-size=2097152
+        -Wl,-z,stack-size=524288
         -lwasi-emulated-process-clocks
         -lwasi-emulated-signal
     )
--- a/qjs.c
+++ b/qjs.c
@@ -798,6 +798,10 @@
         JS_SetMemoryLimit(rt, (size_t)memory_limit);
     if (stack_size >= 0)
         JS_SetMaxStackSize(rt, (size_t)stack_size);
+#if defined(__wasi__)
+    else
+        JS_SetMaxStackSize(rt, 256 * 1024);
+#endif
     if (dump_flags != 0)
         JS_SetDumpFlags(rt, dump_flags);
     js_std_set_worker_new_context_func(JS_NewCustomContext);
